---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bundled language model
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The files under `buttcrack/data/` are a derived statistical model of English,
built by `scripts/build_language_model.py`. They are not source code and not
secret material: they are letter n-gram counts and a word-frequency dictionary
used to judge whether a candidate decryption reads as English.

The model is derived from two openly licensed upstream sources, both fetched
from PyPI at build time:

  * wordsegment 1.3.1 -- Apache License 2.0
    Supplies the unigram and bigram frequency lists originally compiled by
    Peter Norvig from the Google Web 1T corpus (1/ngram, "Web 1T corpus",
    distributed by the LDC). buttcrack uses the derived frequency tables, not
    the library itself.
      https://pypi.org/project/wordsegment/

  * pyspellchecker 0.9.0 -- MIT License
    Supplies the English word-frequency dictionary from which the 80,000 most
    common words were taken.
      https://pypi.org/project/pyspellchecker/

`buttcrack/data/model_meta.json` records the exact versions, the sampling seed,
the corpus size (25,000,000 words) and the resulting table sizes, so a build is
reproducible and the provenance travels with the data.

Neither upstream project endorses this one. The Apache-2.0 and MIT notices above
are reproduced in summary form; the full license texts are available from the
respective project pages.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bundled non-English language models
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

`french_/german_/italian_/latin/spanish_{bigrams,trigrams,quadgrams}.txt.gz`
under `buttcrack/data/` are letter n-gram count tables for five more
languages, used by `--language` to judge candidate plaintexts that are not
English.  They carry no dictionaries: only English ships a word list, so under
the other languages the solver's verdicts rest on n-gram fitness alone (see
the README section on languages for what that means in practice).

The tables are the practicalcryptography.com n-gram counts published by
James Lyons (counts distilled from Wortschaftz corpora), imported by
`scripts/import_ngram_tables.py`, which re-exports them gzipped, drops
non-A-Z grams, and records each table's size and self-entropy in
`model_meta.json` so the scoring thresholds in the code can be checked
against the data that produced them.

  https://practicalcryptography.com/cryptanalysis/letter-frequencies-various-languages/

Mr. Lyons publishes the tables for free reuse; he does not endorse this
project.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bundled M-94 disk set
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

`buttcrack/data/m94_disks.txt` is the standard 25-disk set of the US Army
M-94 / Navy CSP-488 wheel cipher (Major Joseph Mauborgne, 1917; adopted
1922).  The alphabets are public: they were engraved on every device
manufactured, and the set has been reproduced in print since Friedman's
day (e.g. Military Cryptanalytics; a convenient online copy with the
history is prc68.com/I/M94.shtml, whose disk 17 -- the one spelling
ARMY OF THE US around its rim -- matches the file letter for letter).
Each disk here is normalised to start at its `A`; disk identity is the
letter that follows (disks B through Z), which is also how keys are
written.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party runtime dependencies
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

None. buttcrack uses only the Python standard library at runtime.
